## Decoding the question
**"Evaluate Pakistan's performance"** asks you to **judge** the 2025 result — not just state the rank but explain what it shows, sub-index by sub-index. **"Identify the socio-economic and political factors"** asks for **causes**, and the question lists four areas: **labour-force participation, education, health and political empowerment** — cover each. Mentioning the **2026** report shows currency.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) The result
In the World Economic Forum's **Global Gender Gap Report 2025**, Pakistan ranked ==148th of 148== economies — **last** — with ==56.7%== of its gender gap closed (about 57% in 2024).

#### ii) The context
The next year's report (==2026==) placed Pakistan ==143rd of 145==, with ==59.5%== closed — its strongest gain in a decade — though still the lowest in South Asia.

#### iii) The argument
Pakistan's 2025 performance was **poor across the board** and **worst in economic participation**. The causes are **socio-economic** — very low female employment, restrictive norms, unpaid care, poverty and low public spending — and **political** — weak will, few women in power and weak enforcement of laws.

### 2. The index
The **Global Gender Gap Index**, published since ==2006==, measures how much of the **gap** between women and men a country has closed in four sub-indices: **economic participation and opportunity**, **educational attainment**, **health and survival**, and **political empowerment**. It measures **gaps**, not levels of well-being, so a country's rank can fall even while it makes some progress, if others improve faster. A score of **100%** would mean full parity; Pakistan's 56.7% means a little over half of the gap had been closed, against a global average of about 69%.

### 3. Evaluation — economic participation
This was Pakistan's **weakest** area. Female **labour-force participation** was only about a quarter of working-age women; women earn far less than men, and very few are managers, legislators or senior officials. In the 2026 report Pakistan still ranked ==144th of 145== on this sub-index. The economic gap alone keeps Pakistan near the bottom.

### 4. Evaluation — educational attainment
Female literacy remains well below male literacy — about ==53%== against ==68%== in the **Census 2023** (the WEF used a figure of about 48.5% for female literacy in 2025, up from 46.5%). More than **25 million** children are out of school, most of them girls. The 2025 report recorded small gains, which became larger in 2026 (education rank ==130th==, up 11 places).

### 5. Evaluation — health and survival
Pakistan's **maternal mortality ratio** was ==186== per 100,000 live births (2019); about ==42%== of women are anaemic; and **son preference** affects the sex ratio and girls' care. This sub-index is compressed for most countries, but Pakistan remains near the bottom.

### 6. Evaluation — political empowerment
The 2025 report found that the share of women among Pakistan's **government ministers** had fallen to **zero** (from 5.9% in 2024). Women's parliamentary presence depends mainly on **60 reserved seats**; only ==11== women won general National Assembly seats in the ==2024== election. The appointment of **Maryam Nawaz** as the first woman **chief minister** of Punjab (==2024==) did not count in the index's national measures.

::: fig grid | Pakistan's performance by sub-index — the 2025 picture and the 2026 update.
@cols 4
Economic participation: weakest; still 144th of 145 in 2026
Education: small gains in 2025; 130th in 2026
Health and survival: MMR 186; anaemia; sex ratio
Political empowerment: no women ministers in 2025; 107th in 2026
:::

### 7. Socio-economic factor 1: low female labour-force participation
Female participation was ==24.4%== in the **Labour Force Survey 2024–25**, among the lowest in the world, with wide provincial gaps (**Punjab** 33.8%, **Balochistan** 14.5%). Most working women are in **agriculture** and the **informal** sector, often unpaid, and their work is under-counted. The ILO has reported one of the world's **widest gender pay gaps** in Pakistan.

### 8. Socio-economic factor 2: norms, mobility, care and poverty

#### i) Norms
**Purdah**, family **honour**, the male-breadwinner ideal and **early marriage** limit girls' schooling and women's work. Many families still see a daughter's paid job as a sign that the men cannot provide, so even educated women often leave work after marriage.

#### ii) Mobility and safety
Unsafe transport and **harassment** keep women from jobs and colleges. Few women drive or own vehicles, public buses are crowded and male-dominated, and a long or unsafe journey is often reason enough for a family to refuse permission.

#### iii) Unpaid care
Without **childcare**, women carry most household and care work. Time-use surveys show Pakistani women spending several times as many hours as men on cooking, cleaning, fetching water and caring for children and the elderly — work that no index counts as economic output.

#### iv) Poverty
Poor families invest scarce resources in **sons**, whom they expect to support them in old age, while daughters are expected to marry and leave. Girls are withdrawn from school first when money runs short, and their health care is delayed.

### 9. Socio-economic factor 3: low public spending
Public spending of roughly **1% of GDP on health** and **around 2% on education** leaves rural women without schools, clinics and female staff, holding back the education and health sub-indices. Where no girls' middle school exists within walking distance, girls' schooling simply ends after primary level.

### 10. Political factors

#### i) Weak political will and representation
Few women hold power; gender equality is rarely a priority; the fall to **zero women ministers** shows how easily women can be left out of cabinet.

#### ii) The enforcement gap
Pakistan has many pro-women laws — on harassment (==2010==), honour killing (==2016==) and rape (==2021==) — but enforcement is weak.

#### iii) Federalism
After the **18th Amendment** (==2010==), women's development moved to the provinces, whose laws and capacity differ.

#### iv) Crises
The ==2022== floods, economic crisis and IMF-linked austerity strained budgets and women's livelihoods.

::: fig cycle | The factors behind Pakistan's 2025 ranking.
@centre Last of 148 in 2025
Low female labour-force participation
Restrictive norms and early marriage
Unsafe mobility and unpaid care
Low spending on health and education
