:::

### 11. After 2025: the 2026 rise
In ==2026== Pakistan rose to ==143rd of 145== (59.5%), with **education** and **political empowerment** each up 11 places. The rise shows that the ranking can move quickly when **girls' education** improves and **women are appointed** to office. But the **economic** sub-index barely changed — the real test. A higher rank earned through appointments can be reversed by the next cabinet; a rise in women's employment is harder won but far more durable.

### 12. Way forward and conclusion

#### i) Way forward
**Women's employment** — safe transport, childcare, enforcement of the harassment law, protected home-based and digital work; **girls' secondary education** and an **18-year marriage age**; **primary health care** and nutrition; **women in cabinet** and senior posts; **enforcement** of laws; and **sex-disaggregated data**.

#### ii) Conclusion
Pakistan's last-place finish in 2025 reflected long-standing gaps in **economic participation, education, health and political voice**, rooted in socio-economic barriers and weak political will. The 2026 improvement shows that policy matters. Sustained gains will depend above all on bringing more women into **paid, decent work**.

## Why this answer scores
- **Evaluates** the 2025 result **sub-index by sub-index**, with data.
- **Identifies** socio-economic and political factors covering all four areas the question names.
- Uses the **2026** report to show currency and to test the analysis.
- A **grid** and a **cycle** figure summarise performance and causes.
